TY  - JOUR
TI  - Effects of adjuvant corticosteroids on pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in non-hiv adults
AU  - Wieruszewski P.M.
AU  - Barreto J.N.
AU  - Frazee E.
AU  - Daniels C.E.
AU  - Tosh P.K.
AU  - Dierkhising R.A.
AU  - Mara K.C.
AU  - Limper A.H.
T2  - Am. J. Respir. Crit. Care Med.
AB  - RATIONALE Although commonly used in clinical practice, the evidence to support the use of corticosteroids as an adjunctive treatment of Pneumocystis jirovecii Pneumonia (PCP) in adults without human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is lacking. METHODS This single-center, retrospective study evaluated non-HIV acutely ill adults treated for PCP at Mayo Clinic Hospital in Rochester, Minnesota between January 2006 and August 2016. The primary outcome compared change in the respiratory component of the Sequential Organ Failure Assessment score (deltaSOFArespiratory) at the earlier of day 5 or hospital discharge between patients who did and did not receive adjunctive corticosteroids. The SOFArespiratory is determined by the PaO2:FiO2 (PF) ratio or SaO2:FiO2 (SF) ratio when an arterial blood gas is unavailable. Cox proportional hazard modeling was used to determine the relationship between corticosteroid use during PCP therapy and 30-day all-cause mortality. RESULTS The 324 included patients had a mean +/- SD age of 62 +/- 16 years, 202 (62%) were male, and 298 (92%) were Caucasian. The majority of individuals received corticosteroids as adjunctive therapy for PCP (n = 279; 86%). Corticosteroid treatment commenced within 48-hours of diagnosis in 251 (89%) patients and within 48-hours of anti-Pneumocystis treatment initiation in 266 (95%) patients. The median daily steroid dose and duration of therapy was 40 (IQR 38, 80) milligrams prednisone equivalents and 7 (IQR 4, 12) days, respectively. The median (IQR) baseline SOFArespiratory was 2 (1, 3), which corresponded to PF (n = 182) and SF (n = 142) ratios of 167 +/- 90 and 349 +/- 99, respectively. The median day 5 deltaSOFArespiratory was 0 (0, 1) and 132 (41%) of patients experienced at least a 1-point improvement in the SOFArespiratory score. After adjusting for baseline SOFA, no association existed between deltaSOFArespiratory and early corticosteroid exposure (p = 0.92). The overall 30-day mortality was 23.1% (95% CI 18.4%, 27.6%) and no relationship was found between use of corticosteroids and survival after adjustment for baseline severity of hypoxemia (p = 0.08). CONCLUSION No associations were noted between corticosteroid use and improvement in either short-term respiratory function or mortality among patients without HIV treated for PCP. Future research is necessary to elucidate a role for adjunctive corticosteroids for PCP in this subpopulation.
